# Rule: Authoring skills

How to write a harness skill so it earns its context. (Adapted from mattpocock/skills'
`writing-great-skills`.)

- **User-invoked vs model-invoked.** A model-invoked skill's `description` loads every turn,
  so keep it only when the agent must reach the skill on its own. Otherwise set
  `disable-model-invocation: true` — the description becomes human-facing and costs no context.
- **Lead with the trigger.** For model-invoked skills, front-load the leading word and list
  triggers; one trigger per distinct branch, synonyms collapsed. Cut identity that the body
  already states.
- **Progressive disclosure.** Three tiers: ordered steps in `SKILL.md` → in-skill reference →
  external files loaded on demand. Push detail down the ladder to keep the top legible.
- **Single source of truth.** Each fact lives in exactly one place; don't restate it across the
  skill, its rules, and the process doc.
- **The no-op test.** Read each sentence and ask "does this change what a capable agent would
  do?" If not, delete it — don't reword it. Most prose that fails should go, not be rewritten.
- **State the positive.** Steer by naming the target behaviour, not by prohibiting the wrong one.
- **Split only when it pays.** Break out a model-invoked skill when it has a distinct leading
  word worth triggering on; split later steps out when their presence tempts premature completion.
